; A BBC Micro Model B+128 with the DFS in mode 7: the emulator's `--machine bbcbplus`
; (emulator/README.md, "bbcbplus"), a program run with `*RUN NAME ARGS` as on the real machine.
; lathe/docs/boards.md, "The BBC Micro", has the recipe; lathe/rt/bbc.lth gives a program the
; interface of lathe/rt/host.lth over the MOS (OSFIND, OSBGET, OSBPUT, OSWRCH, OSARGS).
;
; Memory the program may use, and nothing the MOS owns (the tests run with --bbc-strict):
; zero page $00-$8F, the language's; PAGE to HIMEM, $1900-$7BFF, for the code and then the
; variables; and the language's workspace $0400-$07FF, but for $0400, where the start-up code
; keeps the stack pointer *RUN entered it with, and $0700-$07FF, where the command line is
; (BASIC's input buffer, and the emulator's --bbc-command) until bbc.lth has read its tail. The
; stack is the MOS's: the start-up code leaves it as it is, and main's return, or exit(0) from
; any depth, returns to the MOS.
;
; The file is the code from $1900 (`output raw`); the build writes NAME.inf beside it, which
; gives *RUN the load and execution addresses, both $1900.
cpu       6502
zeropage  $00 $8F
code      $1900 $6BFF
data      $6C00 $7BFF
more      data $0401 $06FF
exit      return $0400  ; the start-up code keeps S at $0400 (bbc.KEEP), and ends with RTS
output    raw
define    BOARD 3       ; which machine, for lathe/host/driver/boards.py: 3 the BBC
define    PORTS 2       ; lathe/src/sys.lth over lathe/rt/bbc.lth
define    BBCFS 0       ; file names as given; 1: the DFS's (lathe/rt/bbc/bbcfs.lth)
